    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d242">

                <pb facs="0269" n="255" sameAs="frus:frus1947v06/pg255"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Japan)/6–2747: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Associate Chief of the Division of Occupied
                        Areas</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schuler</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of the
                        International Missionary Council in New York</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Decker</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-07-11">July 11, 1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Reference your telegram June 28 addressed to the Secretary.<anchor n="27"
                        xml:id="d242-f1"/> Department is deeply aware of importance reestablishing
                    cultural ties between Japan and other countries earliest possible date and deems
                    it unfortunate that absence Japanese delegates at International Missionary
                    Council meeting might be interpreted as United States fear of allowing Japanese
                    Christians have friendly intercourse with Christians of other lands. Interchange
                    of persons policy with respect to Germany adopted of course on October 16, 1946.
                    Department is endeavoring every way possible to expedite policy decision with
                    respect to Japan now being considered in Far Eastern Commission. Toward that end
                    your telegram has already been brought to attention of Commission. However
                    successful conclusion of delicate international negotiations on broader issues
                    involved would appear preclude at present time requesting that any exceptions be
                    made to general rule of not permitting movement of Japanese nationals prior to
                    Allied policy decision.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank A. Schuler, Jr.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
